1 — Key issues & suggested changes (prioritized)

Use object identity that’s safe to expose

Don’t use email or sequential IDs in client requests. Use opaque userId (Mongo _id) or UUID. Never return raw emails in public contact lists unless necessary. Add a publicProfile object to User to control exposed fields.

Decide on relationship model: one-sided vs mutual

You currently create Contact with status: accepted. Decide:

Auto-accepted (WhatsApp-style): create single record userId -> contactId with status: accepted.

Request/approval: create pending record and create reciprocal record only when accepted.

I recommend starting with auto-accepted (faster to implement) then add pending if you want friend-request UX.

Authorization checks everywhere

Always confirm the authenticated req.user.id matches Contact.userId for delete/list operations — to prevent manipulation of other users’ contacts.

Prevent user enumeration / privacy leak

When adding a contact, do not reveal whether a given user exists through error messages. Use generic errors where appropriate (or rate-limited responses). But because contacts require that target exists, return 404 for not found — acceptable if you accept the privacy tradeoff. Consider a search endpoint requiring stronger rate-limiting.

Rate limiting & abuse control

Add rate limits on /contacts/add to prevent spam. Log repeated failed attempts.

Indexes & pagination

Add DB indexes on Contact.userId and Contact.contactId. Implement pagination for GET /contacts (limit & skip or cursor).

Soft delete vs hard delete

Consider soft delete (removedAt) for audit and undo. If GDPR concerns exist, support hard delete.

Presence & Socket.IO events

Standardize presence events and how status gets updated.

Validation

Validate contactId format (ObjectId) and self-add prevention.

Rate / performance targets

Add explicit pagination default (e.g., 50 contacts per request) and timeouts for presence updates.

2 — Concrete API contracts (final, with edge behaviors)
POST /contacts/add

Request (JSON)

{ "contactId": "64b8e3f..." }


Responses:

201 Created

{ "message":"Contact added", "contact": { "id":"<contactRecordId>", "contactId":"<userId>" } }


400 Bad Request — invalid ObjectId or trying to add yourself

409 Conflict — already a contact

404 Not Found — user not found

429 Too Many Requests — rate limit exceeded

Notes:

Require Authorization: Bearer <JWT>.

Server-side checks: contactId != req.user.id, user exists, contact not present.

DELETE /contacts/:id

:id = contact record id (not the contact's userId). Using record id ensures user can only delete their own contact (and prevents guessing).
Responses:

200 OK { "message":"Contact removed" }

404 Not Found if record not found or not owned by the user

GET /contacts?limit=50&cursor=<cursor>

Default limit=50 (max 200).
Response:

200 OK array of objects:

[
  {
    "contactId":"64b8e3f...",
    "username":"alice",
    "displayName":"Alice A.",
    "avatarUrl":"/images/u64b8...png",
    "status":"online",
    "lastSeen":"2025-09-24T18:30:00Z",
    "contactRecordId":"6423..."
  },
  ...
]


Notes:

status derived in real-time from in-memory presence (Socket.IO). If offline, include lastSeen timestamp.

Implement cursor-based pagination (return nextCursor).

Optional: GET /contacts/search?q=alice

Rate-limit heavily. Returns only public profile fields.

3 — DB Schemas (concise, production-ready)

User (user.models.js)

const userSchema = new Schema({
  username: { type: String, required: true, index: true, unique: true },
  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true, select: false }, // hide by default
  displayName: { type: String },
  avatarUrl: { type: String },
  lastSeen: { type: Date },
  publicProfile: {
    about: String,
    allowSearch: { type: Boolean, default: true }
  }
}, { timestamps: true });


Contact (contact.models.js)

const contactSchema = new Schema({
  userId: { type: ObjectId, ref: "User", required: true, index: true },
  contactId: { type: ObjectId, ref: "User", required: true, index: true },
  status: { type: String, enum: ["accepted","pending","blocked"], default: "accepted" },
  removedAt: { type: Date, default: null }
}, { timestamps: true });

contactSchema.index({ userId: 1, contactId: 1 }, { unique: true });


Notes:

Unique compound index prevents duplicate records.

4 — Socket / Presence contract (server-side responsibilities)

Socket.IO events:

Client emits presence:online after connecting and authenticating socket (include token)

Server emits presence:update to affected contacts:

{ userId: "u1", status: "online", lastSeen: null }


When socket disconnects: server sets lastSeen in DB and emits presence:update -> status: offline, lastSeen: <ts>.

How to implement presence:

Maintain in-memory Map<userId, Set<socketIds>> to support multi-device presence.

Presence broadcast: when user goes online/offline, query their contacts list and emit only to those users (to minimize broadcasts).

5 — Controller pseudocode (addContact)
async function addContact(req, res) {
  const userId = req.user.id;
  const { contactId } = req.body;

  if (!isValidObjectId(contactId)) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'invalid id' });
  if (contactId === userId) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'cannot add yourself' });

  const target = await User.findById(contactId).select('_id username displayName publicProfile');
  if (!target) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'user not found' });

  const exists = await Contact.findOne({ userId, contactId });
  if (exists) return res.status(409).json({ error: 'already a contact' });

  // Insert; if using auto-accept:
  const contact = await Contact.create({ userId, contactId, status: 'accepted' });

  // Optionally notify target via Socket.IO that they were added (only if privacy policy allows)
  socketService.notifyUser(contactId, 'contact:added', { from: userId, username: req.user.username });

  return res.status(201).json({ message: 'Contact added', contactRecordId: contact._id });
}

6 — Tests & Validation (what to cover)

